# Dubizzle Car Scraper

Scrapes used-car listings from `https://uae.dubizzle.com/motors/used-cars/`, stores
them in SQLite via Prisma, and rebuilds the static `index.html` front end.

## Fresh clone

```bash
npm install
npm run db:setup      # creates prisma/prisma/dev.db from migrations
npm run db:seed       # restore the archived scrapes into the DB
npm run export:all && npm run sync:index   # build index.html
```

A fresh clone has **no database and an empty `Scraper/unprocessed/`**, so there is
nothing for `npm run db:save` to import. The archived scrape runs live in
`Scraper/processed/`; the restore step moves them back into the import queue and
loads them:

```bash
npm run db:restore         # dry-run: report what would be restored
npm run db:restore:apply   # move processed/ -> unprocessed/
npm run db:save            # import the queue
```

`db:seed` chains all three. Flags accepted by `db:restore`:

| flag | effect |
| --- | --- |
| `--apply` | actually move the files (without it, dry-run only) |
| `--copy` | copy instead of move, keeping the archive in place |
| `--limit N` | restore only the first N runs |

Each archived filename carries its original scrape timestamp
(`<ISO>-<count>.json`), so restored rows come back with their real `last_updated`
values and the queue imports oldest-first, exactly like a live run. The archive is
large (~2.8 GB / 159 runs), and `--apply` **moves** rather than copies so it does
not double the disk usage — pass `--copy` if you want to keep both.

Then, for current data rather than the archive:

```bash
npm run scrape        # needs a valid Algolia key, see "If the key rotates"
```

---

## Daily use

```bash
npm run scrape        # scrape -> save to db -> reclaim -> export -> rebuild index.html
```

Individual steps:

| command | what it does |
| --- | --- |
| `npm run scrape` | full pipeline (below) |
| `npm run scrape:count` | scrape but only report counts, write nothing (`--dry-run`) |
| `npm run scrape:test` | unit tests + a live Algolia smoke test |
| `npm run test:scrape` | unit tests only, no network |
| `npm run test:db` | save-to-db regression tests (throwaway database) |
| `npm run test:db-duplicates` | just the duplicate-listing regression test |
| `npm run scrape:repair -- <file.json>` | fix schema-invalid fields in an existing scrape file |
| `npm run db:setup` | create/upgrade the database from migrations |
| `npm run db:seed` | restore the archive into the DB (fresh clone) |
| `npm run db:restore` | dry-run of the archive restore |
| `npm run db:save` | import `Scraper/unprocessed/*.json` into the DB |
| `npm run db:reclaim` | `VACUUM` the database (`reclaim-db-space.sql`) |
| `npm run db:compact` | report/drop the unused Car indexes (no data lost) |
| `npm run db:purge -- --older-than 90` | delete listings not seen recently (dry-run by default) |
| `npm run db:size` | audit table sizes, staleness and history depth |
| `npm run export:all` | dump every car from the DB to `all-cars-from-db.json` |
| `npm run sync:index` | rebuild `index.html` from `bootstrap.html` + the export |
| `npm run migrate:template` | convert a `bootstrap.html` that still has inline JSON |
| `npm run studio` | Prisma Studio |

---

## Why the scraper was rewritten

The previous scraper drove a headless browser to
`https://uae.dubizzle.com/motors/used-cars/` and parsed
`#__NEXT_DATA__`. Two things killed it:

1. **Imperva / Incapsula now challenges the requests.** Every request to
   `uae.dubizzle.com` gets a `200` containing a *"Pardon Our Interruption"* page
   plus an `_Incapsula_Resource` iframe and an **hCaptcha** challenge, instead of
   the listing HTML. This is triggered by IP/bot scoring, so it hits headless
   browsers *and* plain HTTP, and it is not fixable with headers or user agents.
   You can see it live: `curl https://uae.dubizzle.com/robots.txt` works (that one
   path is served by `Server: Dubizzle`), while every other path returns the
   challenge.
2. **The page no longer carries the data where the old code looked.** The listing
   page's payload moved: the redux action the scraper read
   (`listings/fetchListingDataForQuery/fulfilled` → `.payload.hits`) is not always
   populated the same way, and the scraper's card selectors had drifted
   (`listing-location` → `.mui-style-t0mppt` is gone). That is why recent scrapes
   wrote tens of thousands of rows with `price`, `make`, `model`, `mileage`,
   `spec` all `null`.

### The new approach

**The listing page is only a shell - it fetches its data from Algolia.** The page
payload ships the credentials it uses:

```
algolia_app_id:  WD0PTZ13ZS
algolia_app_key: cdd839b4fdac840289e88633779e8634   (public search-only key)
algoliaIndexName: motors.com
```

Algolia is a third-party search service, so querying it directly **does not touch
Dubizzle's WAF and never shows a captcha**. The new scraper:

- posts to `https://WD0PTZ13ZS-dsn.algolia.net/1/indexes/motors.com/query`
- filters `category_v2.slug_paths:"motors/used-cars"` (~34.5k live listings)
- pages 1,000 hits at a time
- **partitions** the category by make (and by year when needed) because a single
  Algolia query can only reach the first 10,000 hits

It is also *better data* than before: price, mileage, year and the full spec sheet
arrive as structured fields rather than parsed DOM, and each listing carries all
of its photos.

Measured on a full run: **~34,500 cars in ~3 minutes**, with 100% coverage of
`price`, `mileage`, `year`, `make`, `model`, `detailPageUrl` and `added`.

---

## Options

```bash
node Scraper/scraper.js --limit 200      # smoke test (stops early)
node Scraper/scraper.js --out tmp.json   # custom output file
node Scraper/scraper.js --dry-run        # fetch + validate, write nothing
node Scraper/scraper.js --quiet          # summary only
```

Tuning via environment variables (see `Scraper/algolia-config.js`):

| variable | default | meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `SCRAPE_CONCURRENCY` | `4` | parallel Algolia requests |
| `SCRAPE_RETRIES` | `5` | retries per request (exponential backoff) |
| `SCRAPE_MIN_CARS` | `1000` | abort instead of writing a suspiciously small file |
| `ALGOLIA_APP_ID` / `ALGOLIA_API_KEY` / `ALGOLIA_INDEX` | see above | credential override |

Output goes to `Scraper/unprocessed/<timestamp>-<count>.json`, which is exactly
what `npm run db:save` consumes.

## Safety behaviour

The scraper refuses to write output that would poison the database:

- any failed partition → exits non-zero and writes **nothing** (no partial files)
- fewer than `SCRAPE_MIN_CARS` cars → exits non-zero, writes nothing
- 401/403 from Algolia → tells you the key rotated instead of writing empty rows
- **type validation against the Prisma schema** → if any field would not satisfy
  `prisma.car.create()` (e.g. a spec value arriving as an object), the run exits
  non-zero and writes nothing, naming the offending fields

That last check exists because an invalid type only surfaces *inside*
`save-to-db.js`, after thousands of rows have already been inserted, and leaves
the import half-done. Catching it before the file is written means `npm run
scrape` either imports cleanly or fails loudly with nothing to clean up.

## Repairing an existing scrape file

If you have a file produced by an older mapper (for example one containing
`motorsTrim: { id: null, name: null }`, which makes Prisma throw
`Expected String or Null, provided Object`), you can fix it in place instead of
re-scraping:

```bash
node Scraper/repair-scrape.js Scraper/unprocessed/<file>.json --dry-run   # report only
node Scraper/repair-scrape.js Scraper/unprocessed/<file>.json             # fix in place
```

It only rewrites fields whose runtime type does not match the schema, so it is
safe to run repeatedly, and afterwards `npm run db:save` will complete.

## Importing a scrape into the database

`npm run db:save` (or the equivalent step inside `npm run scrape`) reads every
`.json` in `Scraper/unprocessed/`, imports it, then moves the file to
`Scraper/processed/`.

It is batched. The original version issued a `findUnique` and then an
`insert`/`update` **per car** - about 70,000 round trips for a 34.5k-car scrape,
which is why the step dominated the pipeline runtime. It now:

1. reads and parses every queued file,
2. loads the existing rows those files could affect with a few chunked
   `findMany` calls (not the whole table),
3. decides insert / update / skip in memory,
4. bulk-inserts new cars and their history with `createMany`,
5. applies updates with a single `updateMany` per distinct change-set.

A car whose scraped data is identical to what is stored costs **no SQL at all**,
so a re-import that finds nothing new does not touch the database.

| 5,000 cars | before | after |
| --- | --- | --- |
| fresh insert | ~2 min | **2.8 s** |
| re-import, no changes | ~2 min | **~1 s** |
| 25 cars with changed price/mileage | ~2 min | **~4 s** |

That is ~0.55 ms/car inserting, so the full 117k-row table imports in well under
a minute instead of ~45.

### Behaviour is verified against the old implementation

`Scraper/diag/differential-save.js` runs the **pre-optimization** script (pulled
from git) and the new one over identical fixture files against separate throwaway
databases, then diffs every `Car` and `CarHistory` row. Current status: all
scenarios identical, covering

- newer data wins; prices and mileage update and are recorded in `CarHistory`
- a brand-new listing appearing later is inserted
- nulls never overwrite stored values
- an older (or equally old) file changes nothing
- `1`/`0` and `"2016"`-style values are coerced to the right column types

Two deliberate differences from the old script, both of which were bugs:

- **Unknown keys in a scraped record are ignored.** The old script spread the
  whole record into Prisma, so any extra field (e.g. `categorySlugPaths`, `site`)
  made the import throw `Unknown argument` and abort.
- **`1`/`0` are accepted for Boolean columns** instead of erroring with
  `Expected Boolean or Null, provided Int`.

Also note `last_updated` still means **"when this car's data last changed"** - an
unchanged car keeps its old timestamp rather than being refreshed on every run.
That matters: `last_updated` currently has only ~141 distinct values across
117k cars, so bumping unchanged rows would collapse thousands of cars onto one
timestamp and erase the history.

Files move to `processed/` only after their rows are committed, and re-running is
safe: rows already at or newer than the file's timestamp are skipped.

### Duplicate listings inside one file

A scrape file can name the same listing more than once. The Algolia partitions
overlap, so a car reachable from two partitions is returned by both. This is not
rare in the archive - **91 of the 162 runs contain repeats** (146,582 repeated
`listingId`s, about 297k redundant records):

```
2025-10-17T22-32-41-41365.json   41,365 records, 10,906 duplicated ids
2025-10-21T06-37-48-41566.json   41,566 records, 10,186 duplicated ids
2025-10-18T08-33-38-40872.json   40,872 records, 10,163 duplicated ids
```

The importer collapses those repeats before writing, because every write keys on
`listingId`: two inserts of one id made `createMany` fail the whole chunk with
`Unique constraint failed on the fields: (listingId)` (P2002) and aborted
`npm run db:seed` on a fresh clone.

- **First occurrence wins**; a later occurrence may only fill nulls, the same rule
  the cross-file path uses, so a repeat never clobbers real data. Older files do
  contain complementary rows for one listing (one carrying the price, one the
  mileage), and those merge into a single complete row.
- **At most one `CarHistory` row per listing per file**, so a repeated price move
  is one entry in the history, not several identical ones.
- The summary reports them separately as `Merged repeats`, so they are never
  miscounted as inserted, updated or unchanged cars.

The scraper itself already de-duplicates in memory (`seen` in `scraper.js`), so
this is defence for the archived files - which are the only input `db:seed` has.

Covered by `npm run test:db-duplicates` (`Scraper/test-duplicates-in-file.js`):
repeats import cleanly, gap-filling repeats merge, and a later file still applies
updates normally. `Scraper/test-helpers.js` holds the throwaway-database and
queue-parking scaffolding both import tests share.

## Database size

`dev.db` sits right at ~99 MB, and Prisma's own tooling refuses to open a SQLite
file larger than 100 MB (`migrate diff` fails with *"File opened that is not a
database file"*). It is already `VACUUM`ed - `freelist_count` is 0 - so there is no
free space to reclaim; the size is real data. Two levers, one of which throws away
nothing.

### 1. Drop the unused indexes (`npm run db:compact`) - no data lost

The app only ever queries `Car` by `listingId` (the primary key) or orders by
`last_updated`. The nine `@@index` declarations that used to be in
`prisma/schema.prisma` were never used by any query, and together cost ~18 MB.

This is now handled by a migration -
`prisma/migrations/20260923143000_drop_unused_car_indexes` - so it applies
automatically:

```bash
npm run db:setup      # prisma migrate deploy
```

`npm run scrape` also applies migrations as its first step. The migration is
followed by the existing `VACUUM` (`reclaim-db-space.sql`, run via
`npm run db:reclaim`), which is what actually returns the space to the filesystem.

Measured on a copy of the live database: **98.85 MB → 80.99 MB** (saved 17.86 MB,
18.1%), with `car.count`, `findUnique` by `listingId`, `orderBy last_updated`,
`findMany where listingId in (...)`, `updateMany` and `createMany` all still
working, and `PRAGMA integrity_check` returning `ok`. No rows are touched.

`npm run db:compact` reports and, with `--apply`, can drop the indexes for a
database where the migration is recorded as applied but they are somehow still
present. It refuses to act while the migration is pending, since dropping them
early would make the migration fail.

### 2. Purge listings that are no longer live (`npm run db:purge`) - deletes rows

Every scrape writes a full snapshot of the live inventory (~34.5k listings), but
rows are never removed, so the table accumulates delisted cars. At the last audit:

```
cars total           : 117,993
not seen in 7 days   :  82,853  (70%)
not seen in 30 days  :  82,853
not seen in 90 days  :  82,853
not seen in 180 days :  82,853
```

That count is identical at every cutoff, which is the important detail: these are
not gradually ageing out, they are **82,853 listings from before the Algolia
rewrite that have never been re-seen**. Only ~35,140 are actually live.

```bash
npm run db:purge -- --older-than 90            # report only (dry-run is the default)
npm run db:purge -- --older-than 90 --apply    # delete + VACUUM
```

At 90 days that removes 82,853 cars and 122,762 history rows, taking the database
from ~99 MB to **~29 MB**. The export and the page shrink with it:

| | now | after a 90-day purge |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `dev.db` | 98.85 MB | ~29 MB |
| `all-cars-from-db.json` | 115.6 MB | ~34 MB |
| `index.html` | 20.4 MB | ~6 MB |

This is destructive and opt-in: dry-run is the default and it prints exactly how
many cars and history rows it would remove before you pass `--apply`. Do the
index step first - it is free.

## Which script does what

`export-cars-from-db.js` is still required. The data path is two steps and both
are needed:

```
dev.db (SQLite)
   -> export-cars-from-db.js          -> all-cars-from-db.json  (dump from the DB)
   -> update-index-html-with-new-cars.js -> index.html           (compress + embed)
```

`update-index-html-with-new-cars.js` deliberately does **not** read the database:
it takes `bootstrap.html` plus `all-cars-from-db.json` and compresses that JSON
into the page. That keeps the page builder pure - you can rebuild `index.html`
from a saved export with no database - and leaves the export as a plain,
inspectable JSON artifact.

They are not alternatives. `npm run export:all` then `npm run sync:index` is
exactly what the `scrape` pipeline runs at the end.

## If the key rotates

The key above is the public search key every browser receives, so it is not a
secret - but Dubizzle can rotate it. Symptom: the scraper exits with
`Algolia rejected the credentials (403)`.

To refresh it: open <https://uae.dubizzle.com/motors/used-cars/> in a real
browser, then in DevTools → Network look for a request to `algolia.net` or
`algolia.dubizzle.com` and copy the `x-algolia-api-key` header (and
`x-algolia-application-id`). Then either export it:

```bash
$env:ALGOLIA_API_KEY="..."; npm run scrape
```

or copy `Scraper/algolia-credentials.example.json` to
`Scraper/algolia-credentials.json` (git-ignored) and fill it in.

## `prisma db execute` fails on this project

Do not reintroduce `npx prisma db execute` into the npm scripts. On Prisma 5.15
with SQLite it fails before running any SQL:

```
Error: SQLite database error
unable to open database file
```

The database is fine - `prisma validate`, `prisma migrate status`, `prisma studio`
and every `@prisma/client` query work, and the same `VACUUM` succeeds through the
client. It is the CLI's migrate engine. Passing `--schema`, passing an absolute
`--url`, and running from inside `prisma/` all fail the same way.

Because `npm run db:seed` ended with that command, a **fully successful** seed
still exited non-zero. The step is now `Scraper/reclaim-db-space.js`
(`npm run db:reclaim`), which reads `reclaim-db-space.sql` and runs it through the
Prisma Client.

